WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.040
The road to success is paved with enormous
screw-ups, and Earth's Mightiest Heroes have

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.670
had their share of bad luck.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:07.820
How bad, you may well ask?

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:08.940
Well, let's take a gander.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.420
Beware: possible spoilers ahead, if Marvel
Studios ever decides to draw on seriously

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.460
the dumbest stories in its history.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:16.880
"Holy mackerel."

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:22.380
Even with the clunky Captain America costume
and the shoehorned Cap/Thor/Iron Man fight

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.989
scene, 2012's Avengers is still better than
the nightmare that was Avengers #1.

00:00:26.989 --> 00:00:31.269
Brought together by the chicanery of Loki,
the original Avengers consisted of Iron Man,

00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:34.750
Thor, Ant-Man, and the Wasp banding together
to take down the Hulk.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.890
Tony's armor looked like a gold-plated iron
lung on legs.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.000
Thor, still waffling between his personas
as the God of Thunder and surgeon Donald Blake,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.650
keeps his identity a secret by loudly announcing
what he's doing every time he transforms into

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.050
the Asgardian deity.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:52.510
The Hulk runs from battle at one point and,
pretty immediately, disguises himself as a

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.870
super-strong juggling clown robot in a traveling
circus.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.320
Then there's Ant-Man and the Wasp.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.510
Ant-Man berates Wasp for being a woman.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.230
When he and Janet arrive at their first Avengers
meeting, they stand on a projector to make

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:06.810
themselves big enough to talk to instead of
just growing to person size.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:10.970
They try to capture Hulk by having ants dig
a hole under him, and are defeated when Hulk

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.680
blows a fireplace bellows at them.

00:01:13.969 --> 00:01:18.100
Captain America didn't join the Avengers until
four issues into their adventures.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.740
How did Steve Rogers get to the party?

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.340
It was a little problematic.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.940
Thor, Ant-Man, Wasp, and Iron Man were pitted
against Golden Age staple Namor the Submariner.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.080
Namor escapes the tussle and makes his way
to the North Sea, where he finds a group of

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:36.299
"uncomprehending Eskimos" worshiping a petrified
figure as "mighty lord of the frozen ice."

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:40.329
Namor, having been gifted with the strength
of a typhoon and intense crabbiness, breaks

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:44.770
apart the ice and sends the frozen figure
floating into warmer waters, where his frigid

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:45.620
shell melts around him.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:47.780
"You've been asleep, Cap.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.080
For almost 70 years."

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.320
The frozen figure?

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.320
Captain America.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.819
The Avengers pass in their submarine, and
they make Captain America prove that he's

00:01:56.819 --> 00:02:00.950
Captain America by having an all-out brawl
with him while still inside the submarine.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:05.409
Ignoring the fact that they're in a pressurized
tube surrounded by tons of water, they punch,

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:09.830
dive, and Mjolnir their way to clarity until
Wasp shuts the whole thing down by standing

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.220
in front of Cap and basically daring him to
hit a girl.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:18.320
The year was 1988, and frankly, it was time
for superheroes to do something about rock

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:18.860
and roll.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.380
The West Coast Avengers, within spitting distance
of having their book cancelled, found themselves

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.740
drawn into the path of the Defiler, a suburban
dad's idea of a threatening rock star.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.480
While he may have appeared to be an innocent
skull-adorned behemoth of glam rock fronting

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.080
a band called "Corruption of Innocence," he
was secretly up to no good.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:38.991
Defiler was an interdimensional demon using
the seductive power of loud music to draw

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:43.610
in America's youth, throwing them into portals
and draining them of their "good vibrations."

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:47.710
Luckily for all of those easily manipulated
kids, the only thing more powerful than the

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:52.050
nebulously described powers of a heavily tasseled
devil bad guy is Ant-Man.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:56.800
He saves the day using a giant chainsaw, a
giant skateboard, a giant catcher's mitt,

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.120
and a giant amount of faith in the idea that
readers would be into this.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.900
Defiler gets knocked into one of his own portals,
and that's the last we ever heard from him.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:10.300
Thanos is a big jerk, but darned if that didn't
stop the Avengers from recruiting the Mad

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:11.500
Titan's brother, Eros, to the team.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.720
Better known by his superhero moniker, Starfox,
he was a late-in-the-game addition to Earth's

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.720
Mightiest Heroes.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:20.920
Having the more well-meaning sibling of an
unbeatable space god on your side seems like

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:21.900
a good idea on paper.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.780
Can you imagine the sheer power he probably
brought to the fight?

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:29.820
But Starfox, in addition to the usual powers
of super strength, flight, and so on, possesses

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.040
the ability to super-womanize.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.820
He stimulates the pleasure centers in other
beings' brains, making them susceptible to

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:36.360
Starfoxery.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:39.780
He was even put on trial for assault in the
mid-2000s.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:46.600
In the 1990s, comic book companies were in
hot water.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.230
DC had burst the collectors' market with the
"Death of Superman" and Marvel was on the

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.400
verge of bankruptcy.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.790
So they teamed up and DC vs. Marvel was born.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:57.230
It was a 4-issue series of books in which
the two companies, represented by two cosmic

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.510
gods, sent their respective champions into
battle against one another.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:04.460
The ensuing melees saw members of the Avengers
getting slapped around something fierce by

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:05.460
their DC opponents.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.100
Hulk took a beatdown from Superman.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.000
Captain America, like all who face Gotham's
Dark Knight, got hit with a Batarang and fell

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:11.520
down.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.780
Thor did all right, managing to stop Shazam,
then still called Captain Marvel, by preventing

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.480
his magic lightning from transforming him
into his super self, but it was a victory

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.220
that was dampened slightly by the fact that
it was 1996, and we were still 23 years away

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:27.640
from Zachary Levi making most people care
who Shazam was.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:33.680
In the aftermath of DC vs Marvel Comics, the
two gods representing the companies got together

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.840
and created an all new comic book universe,
the Amalgam Universe.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:41.370
All of the two companies' best-selling characters
saw themselves merged into half Marvel/half

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.530
DC hybrid versions of themselves.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:47.180
The results were hit and miss, and generally
more than a little slanted in one character's

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:47.720
direction.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.520
There was Super Soldier, the Captain America/Superman
hybrid that answered the question, "What if

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.820
Superman had all of his powers and also a
shield?"

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:59.200
There was Dark Claw, a Batman/Wolverine combo
that combined Wolverine's powers with Batman's

00:04:59.200 --> 00:04:59.720
costume.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.340
Doctor Strange and Doctor Fate became Doctor
Strangefate.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:03.820
He was a magic guy.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:08.020
In essence, the two biggest comic book publishers
in the world did what Sid from Toy Story did

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:08.540
to his dolls.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:14.280
In 2000, Marvel launched its Ultimate line,
a modern rebooting of all of its classic characters

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:15.680
in a parallel universe.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:21.150
At its best, we got Ultimate Spider-Man, a
series with solid writing and exciting reimaginings

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.640
of the heroes and villains that fans had loved
for decades.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.020
At its worst, there was Ultimatum.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.300
Ultimatum was a crossover event branching
from Mark Millar's perpetually gritty reboot

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.120
of the Avengers, The Ultimates.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:36.450
It was written by Jeph Loeb, the mind behind
classics like Batman: The Long Halloween and

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.050
Superman For All Seasons.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:39.480
The series went off the rails.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:43.390
The Wasp's intestines get eaten out of her
torso, on-panel, by the Blob.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:45.700
Hank Pym bites the head off of the offending
eater.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:49.980
Pym later explodes, Doctor Strange's head
blows up, and Thor martyrs himself.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:51.420
Also, Manhattan drowns.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:51.960
It's grim.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.840
The mid '90s were a tough time for Marvel.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.110
Sales were plummeting and storylines had become
too convoluted for casual readers to keep

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:00.620
pace.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.320
The publisher needed to shake things up.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:03.560
They needed something fresh.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:08.220
Instead of doing that, Marvel handed its comics
to Rob Liefeld and hoped it fixed everything.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.480
In late 1996, Marvel launched Heroes Reborn.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.020
Spearheaded by Liefeld and Jim Lee, it took
the Avengers, along with a few other Marvel

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:20.300
A-listers, and put them in a slightly different
universe with slightly different origin stories.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:24.660
The event also included some redesigns of
the characters that sidestepped typical human

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:25.380
anatomy.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:29.380
Nobody walked away unscathed, but poor Captain
America got the worst of it.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:33.740
His redesign left readers with an illustration
of machismo-fueled body horror destined for

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:34.260
memehood.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.580
Given his charismatic movie persona, it's
possible you've forgotten that Tony Stark

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:40.740
has had some rough moments.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:43.080
For one, the dude's wardrobe was a mess.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.220
Take the Mark 1.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.280
Immortalized in 2008's Iron Man as sort of
a Ned Kelly mech suit, the outfit first appeared

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.160
in comics in Tales of Suspense #39 back in
1963.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:53.100
It wasn't great.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.160
It got the job done, protecting Tony and keeping
his heart going, but some of its other features

00:06:58.160 --> 00:06:59.960
were objectively less super.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.360
For example, it had suction cups on the hands
so Tony could attach to the ceiling.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:12.380
"Not bad."

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.700
Luckily, by the Mark 2, Stark had improved
on his original design significantly by, uh,

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:18.540
painting it gold.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:20.680
It was slow going at first.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:25.110
Tony's suits seemed to follow the Adam West
utility belt tradition of doing whatever specific,

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:26.710
ridiculous thing needed doing.

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:29.389
He spent a good time getting around on roller
skates, for one.

00:07:29.389 --> 00:07:33.570
He built a scuba suit that shot ink like a
squid, and of course, there was the cartoonishly

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.300
proportioned YT1 armor.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:39.840
Imagine you've been invited to hang out at
a professional level with a super soldier,

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.140
a god, a billionaire, and a hulk.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:42.920
You?

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:43.980
Can talk to ants.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:44.600
And shrink.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:46.880
This, friends, is the burden on Ant-Man.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:51.000
Hank Pym has a long and sordid history of
decidedly unheroic behavior.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.270
Back in Mark Millar's Ultimates run, an argument
ended with Hank hitting his wife, dousing

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.990
her in bug spray, and setting his ants on
her.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:03.250
Before that, there was Avengers #213, where
Pym, now calling himself Yellowjacket, backhanded

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:05.650
The Wasp when she pointed out that he was
acting crazy.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:09.820
He eventually quit the Avengers over it, and
now Scott Lang cosplays as him.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:11.160
"I lost your mother.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:16.380
I didn't mean to lose you, too."

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.660
Scarlet Witch has had a doozy of a time trying
to start a family.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:26.720
Her most well publicized misadventure into
domesticity came with 2005's House of M event,

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.900
in which she tried to create her own kids
wholecloth by reconstructing the fabric of

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:31.440
reality.

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:33.990
Weirdly, that wasn't the most surreal example.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:38.250
In the '80s, Wanda Maximoff tried to create
a pair of kids with magic, with her as the

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.210
mother and Vision as the father.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:44.620
What she got ended up being an unholy and
overly complex manifestation of shards of

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:45.900
a demon soul.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:48.720
Then the supervillain Master Pandemonium got
his hands on them.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:50.880
Or rather, they got on his hands.

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:54.940
Long story short, Master Pandemonium is a
demonic antagonist with the power to turn

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:56.250
his arms into demons.

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:59.960
The minute he got within reach of Wanda's
kids, he grafted them onto the ends of his

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:01.700
elbows, and he had baby arms.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:05.750
Yes, one of the Avengers' foes once fought
the team with babies for arms.

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:09.380
Incidentally, versions of Wanda's kids eventually
went on to join the Young Avengers.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:16.050
With the meteoric rise of the MCU over the
last decade, the casual viewer might ask themselves,

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:18.390
"Why didn't anyone try to make Marvel movies
sooner?"

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:21.400
A few filmmakers did, and they were awful.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:26.260
Captain America had some serials in the 1940s,
and those have sort of a nostalgic charm.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:29.390
After that, it's tough to find an Avenger
with a worthwhile movie outing.

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:34.910
There was the 1977 Spider-Man, starring a
man in pajamas and enormous web shooters desperately

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:36.140
scurrying up buildings.

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:41.940
Thor appears in 1988's The Incredible Hulk
Returns, the TV movie continuation of the

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:44.900
Incredible Hulk TV series, with a Thor adorned
in fur.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:49.270
There's also the movie-length pilot for a
Doctor Strange series starring a young Jessica

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:54.460
Walter, a.k.a. Lucille Bluth, as Morgan le
Fay and Peter Hooten as the good Doctor.

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:58.400
And who could forget that David Hasselhoff
played Nick Fury a mere ten years before Sam

00:09:58.400 --> 00:09:59.570
Jackson took on the role?

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:00.940
A lot of people, probably.

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:05.580
"I always heard living well's the best revenge."

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:09.920
It's not just every single Marvel character
that's searching for redemption; it's Marvel

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:10.460
itself.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:14.060
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:17.260
Plus, even more Looper videos about Marvel
are coming soon.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:20.960
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

